Actually, wait — surprises are files that broke but weren't in the top-10 predictions. So they won't be in the snapshot data at all. What I can do is:

• For hits: count which signals were active (from snapshot). These signals are "working" — they contributed to correct predictions.
• For **surprises**: we don't have signal data (they weren't in top-10). This means signals that should have flagged these files were missing/underweighted.

A simpler and more robust approach: focus on signal effectiveness from the data we actually have:
• Count how often each signal appears in hit files across all validation events
• Count how often each signal appears in top-10 predicted files that were NOT hits (false positives / "clean" files)
• A signal that shows up more in hits than in clean predictions is more useful

Let me think about this differently. The snapshot stores the top-10 predicted files with their signals. The validation events tell us which of those predictions were correct (hits) and which files broke that weren't predicted (surprises). 

For learning:
• **Signal present on hit file**: signal contributed to a correct prediction → reward
• **Signal present on predicted file that was clean (not a hit)**: signal contributed to a false alarm → no change or slight penalty  
• Surprise files have no signal data but their existence means some signals should be stronger

The cleanest approach: compute per-signal hit rate vs overall hit rate.

